NNAAMMEE
     cchhggrrpp — change group

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     cchhggrrpp [--ffhhvvxx] [--RR [--HH | --LL | --PP]] _g_r_o_u_p _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The cchhggrrpp utility sets the group ID of the file named by each _f_i_l_e oper‐
     and to the _g_r_o_u_p ID specified by the group operand.

     The following options are available:

     --HH      If the --RR option is specified, symbolic links on the command line
             are followed and hence unaffected by the command.  (Symbolic
             links encountered during traversal are not followed.)

     --LL      If the --RR option is specified, all symbolic links are followed.

     --PP      If the --RR option is specified, no symbolic links are followed.
             This is the default.

     --RR      Change the group ID of the file hierarchies rooted in the files,
             instead of just the files themselves.  Beware of unintentionally
             matching the “_._.” hard link to the parent directory when using
             wildcards like “.*”.

     --ff      The force option ignores errors, except for usage errors and does
             not query about strange modes (unless the user does not have
             proper permissions).

     --hh      If the file is a symbolic link, the group ID of the link itself
             is changed rather than the file that is pointed to.

     --vv      Cause cchhggrrpp to be verbose, showing files as the group is modi‐
             fied.  If the --vv flag is specified more than once, cchhggrrpp will
             print the filename, followed by the old and new numeric group ID.

     --xx      File system mount points are not traversed.

     The --HH, --LL and --PP options are ignored unless the --RR option is specified.
     In addition, these options override each other and the command's actions
     are determined by the last one specified.

     The _g_r_o_u_p operand can be either a group name from the group database, or
     a numeric group ID.  If a group name is also a numeric group ID, the op‐
     erand is used as a group name.

     The user invoking cchhggrrpp must belong to the specified group and be the
     owner of the file, or be the super-user.

     If cchhggrrpp receives a SIGINFO signal (see the ssttaattuuss argument for stty(1)),
     then the current filename as well as the old and new group names are dis‐
     played.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/group  group ID file

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The cchhggrrpp utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     In previous versions of this system, symbolic links did not have groups.

     The --vv and --xx options are non-standard and their use in scripts is not
     recommended.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     chown(2), fts(3), group(5), passwd(5), symlink(7), chown(8)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The cchhggrrpp utility is expected to be IEEE Std 1003.2 (“POSIX.2”) compati‐
     ble.

